WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.110
Film Courage: How old were you when you arrived
in Los Angeles?

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:04.630
Choice Skinner, Actor, Director and Acting
Teacher: Oh, that’s a good one.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:10.070
Ooh…I don’t want to lie or get it wrong…I
was….

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.339
Film Courage: What hit song was out?

00:00:12.339 --> 00:00:15.360
Choice Skinner: That’s a good one.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:16.360
That I remember.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.930
Aaliyah’s ‘Are you that somebody?’

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:25.400
I remember that being on…listen moving to
Los Angeles was like a serious culture shock.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:32.000
Okay, because I came from Atlanta/Augusta
And lived there for years and then I had this

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.620
R and B artist that flew me out around Thanksgiving
time and we were working on this second album

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.950
(which never saw the light of day unfortunately).

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:46.640
But I remember going down the 101 because
the 101 always freaked me out.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:52.970
It was just like I’m not used to these types
of freeways and I remember ‘Are you that

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:56.580
somebody’ by Aaliyah being on…because
I thought it was so odd that baby “Ahhha”

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.040
in the song.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.110
I’m like Timbaland is crazy.

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.300
He’s got this baby going in there but this
is a dope song.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.540
So yeah…whatever year that was…1998?

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.200
I might have been?

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.120
That was 20 years ago?

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.960
So I probably was 28.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.200
27-28.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:28.070
Film Courage: Okay, what were your expectations,
when you were driving down the 101 (by the

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:29.870
way, the 101 scares me, too.

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:31.390
So I can see why.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:32.970
But it’s also invigorating at night.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.210
It’s so beautiful.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:37.380
And there’s a sense of possibility you’re
like “Wow.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.740
This is Los Angeles.”

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.050
But still it is scary.

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:43.260
What were your expectations at that time?

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:48.770
Choice Skinner: I thought in a year’s time
I was going to have probably five hit records

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:49.770
on the radio.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:58.940
I was going to win an Oscar (not an Oscar,
a Grammy) and I would be able to buy my mom

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:00.640
a house.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:06.409
Yeah…that was a rough time because for how
many years that I have been working on doing

00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:13.530
music and having the success that I did have
and living in Georgia and having my songs

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.959
on the radio and stuff, when I moved to LA
that didn’t happen.

00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:19.510
And it was just a rough time.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:23.170
I mean I was living with the artist’s family.

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:30.980
I didn’t have my own place and it was hard
finding my place in that company.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:37.470
And I was seeing where things were going to
be at that time.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:44.140
So what you had on one hand, I had expectations
on where I wanted it to go but I was also

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:46.810
seeing where it wasn’t going to go.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:51.030
And that’s tough especially when you are
working with someone so talented because you

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.640
say “Hmmm.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.150
I see where you’re headed and it’s not
good.”

00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:01.650
And if that’s where you’re headed what’s
going to happen to me?

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:02.680
Everything happens for a reason.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:06.980
You know I look back at my music career and
I say man…and I still love music.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:11.930
If I had an opportunity I probably would work
with somebody and come out with an amazing

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:12.930
record.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:19.819
But I didn’t get the love that I get in
film, I didn’t get that love in music.

00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:23.400
And so you have to go where the love is reciprocated.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.849
As much as I love music, it wasn’t loving
me.

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:28.470
As much as I love film, it loves me.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:32.840
And that to me…you know it’s like any
relationship (you know what I mean?).

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.480
It can’t be one sided.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:40.940
So my expectations were I assumed I would
have at least five songs on the radio.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.980
I was that good of a music producer and a
songwriter.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:49.700
But it wasn’t meant to be and I had to accept
that, I had to accept that.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:53.810
And making that transition helped me to move
into Film and Television.

00:03:53.810 --> 00:04:01.690
Film Courage: We talked about early flexibility
and it goes back to Darwinism is the species

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:03.520
that survive are the one that adapt.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:09.940
So you’re talking about you saw even though
you loved music, it was everything you wanted

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:15.840
to be, what was that moment like when you
realized…I mean how did you even see that?

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:21.489
How did you see as much as I love this, this
is me, it’s not going to happen.

00:04:21.489 --> 00:04:26.629
Choice Skinner: I’m probably speaking the
tale of many people who got to that point

00:04:26.629 --> 00:04:31.229
and quit the industry rather than making the
adjustment.

00:04:31.229 --> 00:04:38.539
I think there is a point to where everyone
gets to in their life and goes listen, because

00:04:38.539 --> 00:04:40.539
it’s really all about love.

00:04:40.539 --> 00:04:44.389
All we are looking for as human beings is
love, we are all looking for it.

00:04:44.389 --> 00:04:49.029
Some of us we find it in entertainment, some
of us we find it from our children, from the

00:04:49.029 --> 00:04:52.710
spouse that we’re with, we find it from
our family, we find it from God, we find it

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:53.710
from religion.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:57.830
We find it the way that we need to find it.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:03.360
And when it’s not reciprocated, it’s a
very powerful and painful thing.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.569
It was a cathartic moment for me.

00:05:07.569 --> 00:05:11.919
I remember being on my knees and just crying.

00:05:11.919 --> 00:05:13.970
And saying “God, why did you bring me out
here?

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.830
This is not going to happen.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:17.830
What do I do?

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:19.270
Do I go back August?

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:21.379
Do I got to New York?

00:05:21.379 --> 00:05:22.379
What do I do?

00:05:22.379 --> 00:05:23.930
It’s not happening, I’ve been struggling.”

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:24.990
I was a personal trainer.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.439
I was working at a gym.

00:05:27.439 --> 00:05:30.240
I did so many different jobs.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:36.759
But I think when you listen to that inner
voice (Oprah calls it the “aha” moment),

00:05:36.759 --> 00:05:41.289
I believe it’s just an epiphany when you
wake up and you go "Maybe that’s just not

00:05:41.289 --> 00:05:43.169
the way it’s supposed to happen.”

00:05:43.169 --> 00:05:47.430
And if you let go, becomes sometimes God will
say “Be still and know that I am God.”

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:49.099
And I’ll go “Okay, what does that mean?”

00:05:49.099 --> 00:05:51.650
What is that level of faith?

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.139
Understanding that if you wake up in the morning
that you are breathing, that you’re eating,

00:05:55.139 --> 00:05:59.669
that you have the capacity of your limbs and
you’re still moving forward and you have

00:05:59.669 --> 00:06:00.690
a roof over your head.

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:02.889
You have food to eat, you have clothes on
your back.

00:06:02.889 --> 00:06:08.029
He’s giving you everything that you need,
so you just have to make that adjustment and

00:06:08.029 --> 00:06:12.779
the adjustment for me was “Okay, music was
not the way.”

00:06:12.779 --> 00:06:20.779
But getting into film (years later) needing
to work on the music which moves any film,

00:06:20.779 --> 00:06:22.009
I had all of that experience.

00:06:22.009 --> 00:06:24.319
And I said “Well, wait a minute?

00:06:24.319 --> 00:06:26.300
That’s what that was for!”

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:30.740
So I snatched that big pearl and I took it
and put it into my big collection of jewels.

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:36.270
And now I realize okay now all of those years
of doing that, no different from me being

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.740
mugged or going through the violence that
I went through in Brooklyn as a kid which

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.139
got me into the martial arts.

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:46.660
All of those years of music, helps me as a
film director.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:51.719
It helps me to teach my actors because they
don’t know 80’s music or 70’s music

00:06:51.719 --> 00:06:54.080
or 60’s, and 50’s, and 40’s, and 30’s.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.669
I know all about that because spent so many
years in music.

00:06:57.669 --> 00:07:01.460
So now when I’m teaching them a little something
about acting, I’ve got a little extra resource

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:06.589
that I can give them through lyrics that they’ve
never heard because all they listen to is

00:07:06.589 --> 00:07:07.589
current music.

00:07:07.589 --> 00:07:09.399
Man…how powerful is that?

00:07:09.399 --> 00:07:14.639
When I’m telling them “Hey listen to Bruce
Springsteen and he was singing this in this

00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:15.789
song.”

00:07:15.789 --> 00:07:19.259
Or listen to Richard Marx when he’s talking
about it don’t mean nothing.”

00:07:19.259 --> 00:07:24.679
And this is attributing to your acting career,
your singing career or your dancing career.

00:07:24.679 --> 00:07:25.720
And they’re going “Wow!

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:26.720
This is amazing stuff.”

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.309
They’ve never heard these songs, I have.

